{"cluster_id": "C-4271", "topic": "500-partition INSERT baseline", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B023-059", "B023-060", "B023-080", "B035-050"], "canonical_claim": "A 10M-row, 500-partition CAS-on-S3 INSERT measured 170.6 seconds versus 22.4 seconds for standard S3, a 7.6-times slowdown. Additionally, The measured INSERT produced `CasRefBatchFlushes=CasRefBatchedMutations=1026`, so the ref-ledger batch size was exactly 1.0, and `CasRefQueueWaitMicroseconds` was 36.2 seconds. Additionally, The CAS-on-S3 500-partition INSERT measured 170.6 seconds versus 22.4 seconds for standard S3, with `S3WriteMicroseconds` at 117.5 seconds, `CasRefQueueWaitMicroseconds` at 36.2 seconds, and `CasRefBatchFlushes=CasRefBatchedMutations=1026`. Additionally, A measured 500-partition wide INSERT was 170.6 seconds on CA versus 22.4 seconds on standard S3, despite fewer PUTs; the CA cost was dominated by serial per-part ref commits, serial per-blob uploads, manifest GET validation, and speculative `deduplication_head_first_min_bytes = 1 MiB` HEADs.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-22-cas-batched-part-commit-v2-design.md#problem", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-22-cas-parallel-write-path-design.md#problem", "utils/ca-soak/scenarios/BACKLOG.md#L2886-L2963"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4272", "topic": "The parallel-write validation success criterion is CasRefBatchFlushes", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B023-096"], "canonical_claim": "The parallel-write validation success criterion is `CasRefBatchFlushes` much less than `CasRefBatchedMutations`, a sharp reduction in `CasRefQueueWaitMicroseconds`, and wall time materially closer to 22.4 seconds.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-22-cas-parallel-write-path.md#task-6-benchmark-validation-backlog-close-out"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4273", "topic": "Stage 1 intentionally leaves CasRefBatchFlushes and CasRefBatchedMutations", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B024-027"], "canonical_claim": "Stage 1 intentionally leaves `CasRefBatchFlushes` and `CasRefBatchedMutations` at an average batch size near 1.0; reducing that serial cross-part metric belongs to stage 2.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-22-cas-writepath-stage1-internal-design.md#testing"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4274", "topic": "wide-insert stage-1 improvement", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B024-034", "B034-123", "B043-031"], "canonical_claim": "Stage 1 reduced the measured CA INSERT from 58.41 seconds to 30.26 seconds, from 3.0\u00d7 to 1.59\u00d7 versus plain S3, while CPU/wall increased from 0.375 to 1.075 and the top-thread share fell from 72.3% to 14.5%. Additionally, Write-path stage 1 reduced CA wide-insert wall time from 58.41 seconds to 30.26 seconds and CA-vs-plain from 3.0x to 1.59x, while reducing top-thread Real share from 72.3% to 14.5%. Additionally, The wide-insert baseline improved from 58.41 seconds to 30.26 seconds and from 3.0x to 1.59x versus plain S3 after `CasBlobUploadPool` and `fanOutBlobUploads`; top-thread share fell from 72.3% to 14.5%.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/BACKLOG.md#read-write", "docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#turn-writepath-gc-perf", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-22-unattended-rev7-lifecycle.md#stage1-complete"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4275", "topic": "The S41 workload is a deterministic single-node", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B024-063"], "canonical_claim": "The S41 workload is a deterministic single-node `MergeTree` INSERT of 10,000,000 rows, 30 mixed-type columns, 500 partitions, and one part per partition, with merges stopped during measurement.", "issue_ids": ["S41"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-23-cas-wide-insert-baseline.md#what-was-measured"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4276", "topic": "For the S41 baseline, plain S3 took", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B024-064"], "canonical_claim": "For the S41 baseline, plain S3 took 19.44 seconds and CAS-on-S3 took 58.41 seconds for 500 parts and 3.53 GiB of logical data; CAS issued 119,432 PUTs, 134,400 HEADs, and about 54,360 GETs.", "issue_ids": ["S41"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-23-cas-wide-insert-baseline.md#headline-result"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4277", "topic": "In the CAS S41 trace, S3 PUT/upload", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B024-065"], "canonical_claim": "In the CAS S41 trace, S3 PUT/upload response waits consumed 87.2% of Real wall time, HEAD-before-PUT consumed 12.6%, and serialization, hashing, ref-ledger/manifest encoding, and part writing together consumed under 0.2%.", "issue_ids": ["S41"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-23-cas-wide-insert-baseline.md#b-cost-centers"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4278", "topic": "The S41 CAS upload waits were concentrated", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B024-066"], "canonical_claim": "The S41 CAS upload waits were concentrated in one dominant thread holding 72.3% of Real samples; only three threads appeared, CPU-busy time was 37.5% of wall, and `CasRefBatchFlushes` equaled `CasRefBatchedMutations` at 1000 over 500 parts, an average batch size of exactly 1.0.", "issue_ids": ["S41"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-23-cas-wide-insert-baseline.md#c-single-threaded"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4279", "topic": "On the fresh-pool S41 insert, CasBlobHeadFirst and", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B024-068"], "canonical_claim": "On the fresh-pool S41 insert, `CasBlobHeadFirst` and `CasBlobBodyPutAvoided` were both 26,680, showing that deduplication avoided 26,680 blob-body uploads while still paying the HEADs.", "issue_ids": ["S41"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-23-cas-wide-insert-baseline.md#d-dedup-head-gate"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4280", "topic": "The S41 HEAD-before-PUT gate issued 134,400 HEADs", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B024-069"], "canonical_claim": "The S41 HEAD-before-PUT gate issued 134,400 HEADs, or 268.8 per part, and accounted for 12.6% of CAS Real wall time; plain S3 issued no HEADs.", "issue_ids": ["S41"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-23-cas-wide-insert-baseline.md#d-dedup-head-gate"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4281", "topic": "The conservative fence-not-rescue behavior costs one self-remount", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B024-111"], "canonical_claim": "The conservative fence-not-rescue behavior costs one self-remount cycle for an ambiguous landed renewal, including about 36.5 seconds of fenced writes with default settings; it requires a timed-out-yet-applied conditional PUT and is expected to be rare on healthy backends.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-24-cas-mount-lease-self-race-fix-v2-design.md#phase-a"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4282", "topic": "The S41 quiescence checks passed 15/15 verdicts", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B025-058"], "canonical_claim": "The S41 quiescence checks passed 15/15 verdicts, forced GC reduced residual `unreachable` to zero in one round, detailed `ca-fsck` reported `dangling = 0`, and peak `MemoryResident` was 1.99 GB.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-24-cas-wide-insert-stage1-effect.md#correctness-at-quiesce"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4283", "topic": "meta_pool_wait must report explicit jobs_scheduled and jobs_completed", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B026-023"], "canonical_claim": "`meta_pool_wait` must report explicit `jobs_scheduled` and `jobs_completed` metrics because work scheduled on the meta pool runs on other threads and is absent from the fold thread's `ProfileEvents` delta.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-25-cas-followups-detector-introspection-s42-forceclaim.md#task-6-the-phase-timer-and-the-ten-round-level-phases"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4284", "topic": "Across historical firings, 338,559 objects were missing", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B026-058"], "canonical_claim": "Across historical firings, 338,559 objects were missing from the fold scan and 58 were missing from the pre-fold scan; only the latter direction is not explained by concurrent deletion because the fold scan demonstrably saw the objects.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-26-list-incompleteness-investigation.md#shape"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4285", "topic": "manifest re-read redundancy", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B026-066", "B034-100", "B036-056", "B042-034", "B043-059", "B050-082"], "canonical_claim": "Measured GC performance found `fold_ref_intake` linear in the ref-log backlog at 256 logs/s, with round trips equal to `logs + 2 \u00d7 edges`; 39.6% of intake manifest fetches were cross-transaction re-reads, making a round-scoped body cache a potential optimization. Additionally, A round-scoped cache could remove 39.6% of manifest-body edge re-reads, measured at approximately 597,000 redundant edges and about 600 seconds on the 404,000-log round. Additionally, Across 959 decoded ref-log transactions, the worklog measured 7,565 manifest edges over 4,573 distinct manifests; 2,991 manifests carried both an add and a remove edge, so 39.6% of intake manifest fetches were cross-transaction re-reads. Additionally, The measured 959-transaction residual pool contained 7,565 edges over 4,573 distinct manifests, so 39.6% of intake manifest fetches were re-reads; intra-transaction redundancy was exactly zero. Additionally, In one fold window, 39.6% of manifest fetches were re-reads: 7565 edges came from 4573 distinct manifests, with zero intra-transaction redundancy and cross-transaction add/remove overlap. Additionally, The measured manifest redundancy was 39.6%: 7,565 edges referenced 4,573 distinct manifests, and 2,991 manifests carried both an add and a remove edge in one window.", "issue_ids": ["#22", "T17"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/BACKLOG.md#gc-manifest-reuse-measured", "docs/superpowers/cas/draft-fixes-20260726.md#p2-manifest-cache", "docs/superpowers/cas/open-tasks-20260727.md#t22", "docs/superpowers/cas/todo-20260726.md#L78-L86", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-26-list-incompleteness-investigation.md#adjacent-perf", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/CURRENT.md#L493-L520"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4286", "topic": "GC round and pending-delete cost", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B026-067", "B027-004", "B034-098", "B036-058", "B042-031", "B042-038", "B043-032", "B050-085"], "canonical_claim": "One observed `pending_deletes` phase lasted 77.2 seconds, compared with 243 milliseconds for the next-worst phase, making it a significant uninvestigated performance cost. Additionally, A measured GC round reached 1830 seconds in `fold_ref_intake` while folding 404,065 logs, and one `pending_deletes` occurrence reached 77.2 seconds. Additionally, A 404,065-log GC round took 1,829.6 seconds for 3,420,091 serial requests, with the large-round cost near 0.5 ms per request. Additionally, A `pending_deletes` occurrence took 77.2 seconds while the next-worst phase took 243 milliseconds, consistent with roughly 150,000 serial `deleteExact` calls at approximately 0.5 ms each. Additionally, One observed `pending_deletes` phase took 77.2 seconds, compared with 243 milliseconds for the next-worst phase in the same 20-minute run.; The GC duration study found round time dominated by serial object-store request latency: a 30-minute round represented about 3.42 million serial round trips at approximately 0.5 ms each, with repeated manifest work secondary. Additionally, A 30-minute GC round was measured as 3.42 million serial round trips at approximately 0.5 ms each, with phase time attributed to serial request latency rather than CPU or lock time. Additionally, The GC round-duration study measured 0.465\u20130.535 ms per total S3 request, making a 30-minute round equivalent to 3.42 million serial round trips; 39.6% were redundant manifest refetches.", "issue_ids": ["T9"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/BACKLOG.md#duration-arithmetic", "docs/superpowers/cas/draft-fixes-20260726.md#p1-parallel-fetch", "docs/superpowers/cas/draft-fixes-20260726.md#p3-parallel-deletes", "docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#turn-writepath-gc-perf", "docs/superpowers/cas/todo-20260726.md#L88-L90", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-26-overnight-s42-and-gc-perf-study.md#part-b-why-does-a-gc-round-take-tens-of-minutes", "docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-26-list-incompleteness-investigation.md#adjacent-perf", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/CURRENT.md#L962-L988"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4287", "topic": "mount-fence observability metrics", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-415", "B027-072"], "canonical_claim": "Mode 1 observability uses `CasMountFenceArmed`, `CasSelfRemountCompleted`, and `CasSelfRemountMicroseconds`; Mode 2 uses `CasMountFenceExpired` and `CasMountFenceExpiredMicroseconds`. Additionally, The CAS mount-fence write-grace feature introduces the settings/metrics `write_grace_ms`, `waitForWriteGrace`, `CasMountFenceArmed`, `CasSelfRemountCompleted`, `CasSelfRemountMicroseconds`, `CasMountFenceExpired`, `CasMountFenceExpiredMicroseconds`, `fence_tripped_boot_ms`, and `fence_expiry_reported_boot_ms`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-28-cas-fence-observability-and-write-grace.md#self-review", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-28-cas-fence-observability-and-write-grace.md#task-1", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-28-cas-fence-observability-and-write-grace-design.md#a-profile-events"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-4288", "topic": "Mode 2 outage duration is measured from", "target": "operations/monitoring", "member_ids": ["B027-075"], "canonical_claim": "Mode 2 outage duration is measured from the true expiry instant stored in `fence_expiry_reported_boot_ms` until `setMountDeadline` re-arms the fence, rather than from the first refused write.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-28-cas-fence-observability-and-write-grace.md#task-1", "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-28-cas-fence-observability-and-write-grace-design.md#a-mode2"]}
